ot=2$. Because the functions considered here are not promiscuous, we have $|X_{
ot=2}| \geq 0.1 \cdot n/2$. Because there are two times more stubs than nodes, the nodes in $X_{
ot=2}$ receive $2$ stubs on average. If one of the nodes receives $0$ stubs, the success probability is $0$. Otherwise, at least half of the nodes in $X_{
ot=2}$ have to receive exactly $1$ stub to satisfy the average. Therefore, $l$ has at least $b=0.05n/2$ nodes with degree~$1$. Applying \cref{lem:peelingDegree1} and selecting $c_1 > (7/8)^{0.05} \in (0, 1)$ then concludes the proof.

